# The Moon Weaver's Loom

Long ago, when the world was young, there lived a celestial weaver named Seluna who dwelt in the night sky. She possessed a magical loom made of starlight and silver thread spun from dreams.

Each month, Seluna would weave a magnificent blanket to keep the Earth warm during the cold nights. She began with a single silver thread, creating a delicate crescent that hung in the darkness. Night after night, she worked tirelessly at her loom, adding more luminous threads until the blanket grew full and round—a complete circle of brilliant light.

But Seluna was generous and kind. Seeing the creatures below shivering in darkness, she would unravel her creation thread by thread, sending each strand down to Earth as moonbeams to warm the lands. As she unraveled her work, the moon grew smaller, returning to a slender crescent once more.

The cycle never ends. Seluna weaves and unweaves eternally, her devotion to the Earth unbreakable. This is why the moon waxes and wanes—growing full as she creates her masterpiece, then diminishing as she shares her gift with the world below.

