But Asteria's brother Erebus grew jealous of the praise his sister received. "I too shall decorate the night," he declared, and took up a brush of shadow and ink. While Asteria slept, he painted dark shapes across the sky—connecting her points of light into pictures.

When Asteria awoke, she gasped. Her random punctures now formed patterns: a great bear, a hunter with his bow, a swan taking flight. The creatures below no longer just saw light—they saw stories written in stars.

Asteria was angry at first, but then she understood. Her brother had not ruined her work; he had completed it. From that night forward, they worked together—she piercing holes of light, he painting the darkness between them into meaningful shapes.

